          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a case where two conditions documented together must NOT be linked by the coder?",
          "answer": "Yes. The retrieved coding guidance describes cases where two conditions must be kept apart, though it does not list specific Excludes1 pairs.\n\n**Excludes1 is the strong rule.** The code-structure summary says that Excludes1 means \"not coded here\" in the strong sense. The two conditions cannot both be present, so the codes are never reported together. A coder who sees two conditions documented together must check the Tabular List for this note before linking them. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\n**Excludes2 is weaker.** The excluded condition is not part of the code above it, but a patient can have both, and both may be reported. The two notes look alike, so the coder has to read which one applies. (Same source.)\n\n**The \"with\" presumption works in the opposite direction.** Where the Alphabetic Index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification presumes a relationship without requiring the record to state it. For example, type 2 diabetes with chronic kidney disease is coded as diabetic kidney disease \"unless the documentation says the two are unrelated.\" So to keep two conditions separate under this convention, the record has to say they are unrelated. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*.)\n\n**Combination codes can also replace separate codes.** Where a combination code exists, it is used instead of the separate codes. For example, hypertension with chronic kidney disease moves to a combination code rather than two separate codes. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10*.)\n\n**Symptom codes give way to diagnoses.** Once a diagnosis is documented, the symptom code is usually dropped. The exception is a symptom not routinely associated with that diagnosis, which is reported in addition. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 18*.)\n\nThe corpus gives the rules but not a complete list of Excludes1 pairs. For a specific pair, check the Tabular List in the current official ICD-10-CM release, because the Alphabetic Index does not carry these notes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
